seg-57fb29fdd9d9 = The core compiles unchanged to native, to a Python extension, and to WebAssembly. The Python (<c1/>, PyO3 abi3) and WebAssembly (<c2/>, wasm-bindgen) modules are optional, feature-gated dependencies (<c3/> / <c4/>): the default build and the test suite never compile them, while the dependency-audit gate (<c5/>) does inspect their dependency trees, because those crates ship inside the wheel and the npm package. Both call <c6/>, so every surface returns identical results. The WebAssembly module backs the browser playground in <c7/> and exports thirteen functions: <c8/>, <c9/> (one engine run returning result, chart, summary and table together), <c10/>, <c11/>, <c12/>, <c13/>, <c14/>, <c15/>, the <c16/> / <c17/> shareable-URL (URL: web address) codec, and the three exporters <c18/> / <c19/> / <c20/> that back the playground's export menu. Two further front doors reach the same <c21/>: the <b>MCP (Model Context Protocol) server</b> (<c22/>, a workspace-excluded <c23/> crate exposing fifteen tools — <c24/>, <c25/>, <c26/>, <c27/>, <c28/>, <c29/>, <c30/>, <c31/>, <c32/>, <c33/>, <c34/>, <c35/>, <c36/>, <c37/>, <c38/>; the two example tools read the bundled scenarios through the library's off-by-default <c39/> feature, which only this server turns on) and the <b>JetBrains IDE (integrated development environment) plugin</b> (<c40/>, a Kotlin project that shells out to the <c41/> CLI rather than linking the library).

